/*
    To convert the Semi-Formalized Structure into a Formalized Structure, we will examine the natural language clauses in the Semi-Formalized Structure one by one, and formalize each into a formal clause that is well-formed and well-defined according to the guidelines.
*/

{
    "premises": [
        /*
        The clause "The distinct points P, R, S form a triangle △ P R S" has a potential direct formalization in the guidelines: `formTriangle`.

        We double check the description of the `formTriangle` relation: "mutually distinct points a, b and c form a triangle, where point a and b are on line AB, point b and c are on line BC, point a and c are on line CA. The lines AB, BC, and CA must be distinct".

        The mutual distinctness of points in our target clause matches the description of "mutually distinct points a, b and c". By definition, the 3 points that form a triangle must be non-collinear, which matches the description "The lines AB, BC, and CA must be distinct". Therefore, this relation is indeed equivalent to our target clause.

        `formTriangle` takes (a b c : Point) (AB BC CA : Line) as arguments in order, so we can formalize the clause as follows:
        */
        "formTriangle P R S PR RS SP",

        /*
        The clause "Q and T are distinct points on line QT" has a potential direct formalization in the guidelines: `distinctPointsOnLine`.

        We double check the description of the `distinctPointsOnLine` relation: "points a and b are distinct and on line L". It is indeed equivalent to our target clause.

        It takes (a b : Point) (L : Line) as arguments in order, so we can formalize the clause as follows:
        */
        "distinctPointsOnLine Q T QT",

        /*
        The clause "The distinct lines QT and PS intersect at point T" has a potential direct formalization in the guidelines: `twoLinesIntersectAtPoint`.

        We double check the description of the `twoLinesIntersectAtPoint` relation: "distinct lines L1 and L2 intersect at point i". It is indeed equivalent to our target clause.

        Note that PS is the same line as the line SP we have already declared, so we can use the same variable for both.

        It takes (L1 L2 : Line) (i: Point) as arguments in order, so we can formalize the clause as: `twoLinesIntersectAtPoint QT SP T`.

        The clause "The distinct lines QT and PR intersect at point Q" can be formalized in the exact same way.

        Therefore, we can formalize the next 2 clauses as follows:
        */
        "twoLinesIntersectAtPoint QT SP T",
        "twoLinesIntersectAtPoint QT PR Q",

        /*
        The clause "The line segment UX is inside the triangle △ U V W, and is distinct from the sides of the triangle" doesn't have any potential direct formalization in the guidelines, so we have to formalize it equivalently using available operators, constants, and other formal relations.

        For a line segment to be inside a triangle, the two endpoints of it must be inside the triangle. Since the line is distinct from the sides of the triangle, they two endpoints can't be on the same side of the triangle.

        Since T is the intersection point of QT and PS, we know that T is on line PS, but we still need to gaurantee that T is not outside the line segment PS, and T is not the same point as P or S.

        Similarly, Q is the intersection point of QT and PR, so Q is on line PR, but we still need to gaurantee that Q is not outside the line segment PR, and Q is not the same point as P or R.

        Therefore, the clause we want to formalize is equivalent to "T lies between P and S, and Q lies between P and R", which has a potential direct formalization in the guidelines: `between`.

        We double check the description of the `between` relation: "mutually distinct points a, b and c are collinear and ordered. Point b is between point a and c". It is indeed equivalent to our target clause since it guarantees that the points are mutually distinct.

        It takes (a b c : Point) as arguments in order, with Point b being the one that is between Point a and Point c.

        Therefore, we can formalize the clause as follows:
        */
        "between P T S ∧ between P Q R",

        /*
        The clause "The angles ∠ P T Q and ∠ P Q T are congruent, denoted as ∠ P T Q ≅ ∠ P Q T" doesn't have any potential direct formalization in the guidelines, so we have to formalize it equivalently using available operators, constants, and other formal relations.

        The definition of congruence of angles is that the measure of the angles is equal.

        We notice that, following the instructions, the formal notation of an angle fromed by vertex b and sides a and c is `∠ a:b:c`, so we can directly formalize the clause using the equality relation `=` as follows:
        */
        "∠ P:T:Q = ∠ P:Q:T",

        /*
        The clause "The lines QT and RS are parallel, denoted as Q T ∥ R S" doesn't have any potential direct formalization in the guidelines, so we have to formalize it equivalently using available operators, constants, and other formal relations.

        We notice that this clause is equivalent to "WY and TV do not intersect", which has a potential direct formalization in the guidelines: `intersectsLine`.

        We double check the description of the `intersectsLine` relation: "two lines L and M intersect at some point. They can be the same line". It is okay that `intersectsLine` allows the lines to be the same since we are going to negate it, which guarantees that the lines are mutually distinct.

        The `intersectsLine` relation is defined in the namespace `Line` with the syntax `L.intersectsLine M`, so we can formalize the clause as follows:
        */
        "¬ QT.intersectsLine RS"
    ],
    "conclusions": [
        /*
        The clause "The angles ∠ P S R and ∠ Q R S are congruent, denoted as ∠ P S R ≅ ∠ Q R S" can be formalized in the exact same way as the clause "The angles ∠ P T Q and ∠ P Q T are congruent, denoted as ∠ P T Q ≅ ∠ P Q T".

        Therefore, we can formalize the clause as follows:
        */
        "∠ P:S:R = ∠ Q:R:S"
    ]
}
